The '''economy of [[Burawa]]''' is a state-capitalist [[democratic socialism|socialist]] {{w|mixed economy}} in which the state retains ownership of industries classified as essential while permitting private enterprise in non-essential sectors. It is the fifth-largest economy in the world by {{w|purchasing power parity}} and one of the largest exporters in [[Adonia]]. The [[Burawi Hanti]] is softly pegged to the [[International dollar]] at a ratio of approximately four to one.

The Burawi economy is characterised by a substantial gap between its nominal and purchasing-power-parity valuations. This gap is explained by the state's provision of {{w|housing}}, {{w|healthcare}}, and {{w|education}} at no direct cost to residents, which eliminates the largest categories of household expenditure and reduces the nominal cost of living. Wages in the public sector, which employs between sixty and seventy per cent of the workforce, are lower in nominal terms than comparable private-sector positions, but the absence of housing and healthcare costs means that discretionary spending power is higher than nominal wages suggest.

Burawa maintains very low {{w|unemployment}}, estimated at between two and three per cent of the labour force. The {{w|Gini coefficient}} is approximately 27.5, indicating moderate inequality by international standards. The [[Human Development Index]] is rated at 0.854, placing Burawa in the very-high-development category.

== Historical development ==

{{main|History of Burawa}}

The Burawi economy has undergone several transformations over the course of its history. In the medieval period, the economy was primarily agricultural, centred on the river valleys of the [[Buran River|Buran]], [[Dafar River|Dafar]], [[Hooyal River|Hooyal]], and [[Galbeed River|Galbeed]], with the coastal city of [[Mazar]] serving as a hub for {{w|Mesogean}} maritime trade.

The colonial period, beginning with the founding of [[Lacashe]] in 1516, transformed the Burawi economy. Colonial possessions in [[Abyala]] and North Kaftia provided plantation products, raw materials, and, until 1789, enslaved persons. The {{w|triangular trade}} connecting Kaftia, Abyala, and the Mesogean markets generated considerable wealth for the kingdom and elevated the merchant class to a position of political influence alongside the traditional [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] aristocracy.

During the [[Constitutional Era of Burawa]] (1743–1800), the economy operated under {{w|mercantilist}} principles, with chartered companies and private enterprises coexisting under royal patronage. The [[Early Republic Era of Burawa]] (1832–1865) witnessed the arrival of {{w|railroads}}, {{w|steamships}}, and the beginnings of {{w|textile}} manufacturing. Competing railroad companies constructed an extensive track network across the country during this period, laying the foundation for the freight rail infrastructure that remains in use.

The Industrial Era (1865–1885) and the [[Gilded Age of Burawa]] (1885–1915) saw Burawa emerge as a major industrial power. The industrial heartland developed around [[Badalga]], [[Khar]], [[Lanwaley]], and [[Laaqoray]] in the [[Lake Haro]] basin and [[Buran River|Buran]] valley. {{w|Petroleum}} was discovered in 1914, but the industry remained nascent until after the [[Great Adonian War]].

The [[Socialist Republic of Burawa|socialist revolution]] of 1932 resulted in the nationalisation of essential industries and the establishment of a planned economic sector alongside a regulated private market. The post-war period (1948–1962) saw oil and gas revenues become a significant portion of state income. The Crisis of 1962 prompted economic reforms, including the sale of state land in [[Mazar]] to replenish national reserves and the introduction of 99-year commercial land leases to facilitate private enterprise.

From the 1960s onward, the government pursued a deliberate transition away from dependence on {{w|fossil fuel}} revenues, initially for economic reasons and, from the 1990s, for environmental considerations. The modern economy is diversified across manufacturing, oil and gas, technology, and agriculture.

== Economic structure ==

=== Essential and non-essential industries ===

The Burawi economy is divided into two categories of industry. Essential industries, which the state considers fundamental to national welfare and security, are owned and operated by state enterprises. Non-essential industries are open to private ownership and investment.

Essential industries include public transit, aviation (with the exception of [[Mazar Airlines]], which is grandfathered as a private carrier), pharmaceuticals, military production, nuclear energy production, construction, power transmission, mineral extraction (including oil and natural gas), energy production (including hydroelectric and solar), healthcare, and education.

Non-essential industries include consumer services, retail, hospitality, electronics manufacturing, semiconductor fabrication, telecommunications, and technology platforms. Several of these sectors, including electronics manufacturing, semiconductor fabrication, and telecommunications, were previously classified as essential and were reclassified to permit private participation.

=== State-owned enterprises ===

The Burawi government operates a substantial portfolio of state-owned enterprises across multiple sectors. In transport, [[BuRail|Burawa Railways]] (BuRail) operates the national rail network, and [[Dawati]] serves as the national airline. In the energy sector, [[Burawa Oil]], [[Burawa Natural Gas]], [[Burawa Coal]], and the [[Burawa Energy Corporation]] manage fossil fuel extraction and distribution. The [[National Northern Electric Company]] (NNEC), a subsidiary split from the Burawa Energy Corporation, was partially opened to foreign investment to fund the [[Dimar]] hydroelectric project.

Construction is managed by the [[Central Construction Group of Burawa]] (CCG) and the [[National Northern Burawi Construction Group]] (NNBCG). Mining is conducted by three regional corporations: the [[Burawa Central Mining Corporation]] (covering the [[Buran River|Buran]] valley), the [[Burawi Northern Mining Corporation]] (covering the northern regions), and the [[Burawa-Dimar Mining Corporation]] (covering the [[Dimar]] region).

In healthcare, [[Burawa Health Industries]] manufactures pharmaceuticals and the [[National Health Devices Corporation]] produces medical devices. The [[Burawi Motor Company]] (BMC) is the principal state automotive manufacturer, and [[Burawa Steel]] produces steel for domestic consumption and export. The [[Bank of Burawa]] serves as the central bank, and the [[Burawa National Life Insurance Company]] provides state insurance services.

The [[Burawa Tobacco Distribution Corporation]] and the [[Burawa Alcohol Distribution Corporation]] hold state monopolies on the distribution of tobacco and alcohol products, although private production of both is permitted.

=== Major private companies ===

The largest private enterprises in Burawa operate primarily in the non-essential sectors opened to private participation. [[Gari Automotive]] is the leading private vehicle manufacturer, producing cars and buses in competition with the state-owned [[Burawi Motor Company]]. [[WadaWada]] (commonly known as WW) is a social media platform with a large user base across [[Kaftia]] and among the Burawi diaspora. [[Kaftian Telecom]] is the principal private telecommunications provider, operating in the sector since its reclassification from essential to non-essential. [[Kaftian Cement Industries]] supplies the construction sector alongside state contractors.

[[KBOC]] is a credit card processing company with a significant presence across Kaftia. [[Dhismo Semiconductor]] is a private semiconductor manufacturer that emerged after the reclassification of the sector, producing chips for both domestic and export markets. [[Cirka Aerospace]] manufactures airplane components and parts, supplying both the domestic aviation industry and international clients. [[Cunno Foods]] is a Kaftian snack foods company with distribution across the region.

=== 99-year commercial leases ===

Private enterprises outside [[Mazar]] access commercial land through 99-year leases granted by the state, a system introduced during the 1962 reforms. These leases permit private businesses to develop and operate on state-owned land for the duration of the lease. Mazar is the only city in Burawa where private property ownership is permitted.

== Sectors ==

=== Manufacturing ===

Manufacturing has been a pillar of the Burawi economy since the colonial era, when Burawa served as the primary processing centre for raw materials imported from the [[Abyala]]n colonies. The sector encompasses textiles, shipbuilding, automotive production, and technology manufacturing.

The industrial heartland is concentrated in the [[Lake Haro]] basin and the [[Buran River|Buran]] valley, with [[Badalga]], [[Khar]], [[Lanwaley]], and [[Laaqoray]] serving as the principal industrial cities. The [[Burawi Motor Company]] produces vehicles for both domestic use and export, and [[Burawa Steel]] supplies the construction and manufacturing sectors.

Technology manufacturing is a distinctive feature of the Burawi economy. Strict domestic security requirements, including the [[Burawi National ID]] card system (which employs proprietary {{w|NFC}} technology with private keys stored on separate {{w|CPU}}s to verify identity), have driven the development of a vertically integrated technology manufacturing sector. Burawa produces {{w|DRAM}} and {{w|CPU}}s domestically, though screens and displays are imported. Hardware is exported globally, while the software ecosystem is primarily domestic or regional, constrained by language and regulatory factors.

=== Oil and gas ===

Petroleum was discovered in Burawa in 1914, but the industry remained undeveloped until after the [[Great Adonian War]]. In the post-war period, oil and gas extraction expanded rapidly and became a significant source of government revenue, particularly during the Malaise Era (1948–1962) and the early Reform Era.

Oil and gas extraction is classified as an essential industry and is managed by [[Burawa Oil]] and [[Burawa Natural Gas]], both state-owned enterprises. The sector accounts for a moderate share of total exports, estimated at between ten and fifteen per cent. The [[Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)|Temporary Worker Program]] was introduced in part to address labour shortages in the oil industry as domestic workers shifted toward other sectors.

The government has pursued a deliberate reduction in the economy's dependence on fossil fuels since the 1960s, initially for economic diversification and, from the 1990s, for environmental reasons. Nuclear energy, hydroelectric power, and solar energy have been expanded as alternatives.

=== Agriculture ===

Burawi agriculture operates under two systems. The tenant farming system grants individual farmers and their families free management of their assigned land, subject to a minimum production quota assessed over five-year periods. Produce exceeding the quota may be sold to private industry or to international markets through the [[Import-Export Bank of Burawa]]. The system was designed as a transitional mechanism to maintain food security while facilitating the move from the pre-socialist plantation and {{w|sharecropping}} model.

State industrial farms operate in areas where small-scale farming is less viable or where farming was historically uncommon. These farms are managed by rural administrative authorities under the supervision of the [[Ministry of Agriculture]].

The principal agricultural products are {{w|wheat}}, {{w|rice}}, and {{w|sorghum}} among grains, and {{w|cattle}} among livestock. A small fishing sector operates in [[Lake Haro]], along the Mesogean coast, and on major rivers.

=== Technology ===

The technology sector has developed significantly since the reclassification of electronics, semiconductors, and telecommunications from essential to non-essential industries. The reclassification permitted private companies to enter markets previously reserved for state enterprises, producing several major firms. [[Dhismo Semiconductor]] is the largest private semiconductor manufacturer, producing chips for both domestic consumption and export. [[Kaftian Telecom]] is the principal private telecommunications provider. [[KBOC]], a credit card processing company, has become one of the most widely used payment networks in [[Kaftia]]. [[Cirka Aerospace]] manufactures airplane components for domestic and international clients.

The largest private technology company by market capitalisation is [[Sare]], a ride-hailing and food delivery platform listed on the [[Mazar Stock Exchange]] with a capitalisation of approximately $12 billion and annual revenue of $2.5 billion. [[WadaWada]] (WW), a social media platform, has a substantial user base across Kaftia and among the Burawi diaspora.

The domestic technology ecosystem is shaped by Burawa's strict security requirements, which have fostered a vertically integrated hardware manufacturing industry. The [[Burawi National ID]] system, which employs proprietary {{w|NFC}} technology with private keys stored on separate {{w|CPU}}s, requires full domestic production of core components. This security-driven demand has supported the growth of domestic {{w|DRAM}} and CPU manufacturing, though screens and displays remain imported. Hardware is exported globally, while software development is primarily oriented toward the domestic and Kaftian markets, constrained by language and regulatory factors. Software exports are concentrated among neighbouring states including [[Dakare]], [[Maianka]], [[Sabia]], [[Sarafone]], and [[West Dakare]].

=== Tourism ===

Tourism is a growing sector of the Burawi economy. The country's low cost of living relative to international standards, combined with its Mediterranean coastline, historical sites, and diverse geography, has attracted an increasing number of international visitors.

== Labour ==

=== Employment structure ===

Burawa maintains a very low {{w|unemployment}} rate, estimated at between two and three per cent. Between sixty and seventy per cent of the workforce is employed in the public sector, with the remainder in private enterprise.

Public sector wages are determined by a state wage scale system. All public sector salaries are published and transparent, and employees are guaranteed annual raises provided they meet the requirements of their position. Public sector employees are exempt from income tax, which partially compensates for lower nominal wages relative to the private sector.

Private sector employers are free to set their own wages, which produces a bimodal salary distribution across the economy. Private sector wages are generally higher than public sector equivalents, with the gap varying by industry; technology and finance positions command significantly higher salaries than comparable public sector roles, while retail and service positions are closer to parity.

Burawa does not maintain a national {{w|minimum wage}}.

=== Burawi Work Programme ===

{{main|Burawi Work Programme}}

The [[Burawi Work Programme]] (BWP) is a compulsory national service programme for all Burawi citizens upon reaching the age of eighteen. Participants are allocated to one of five tracks based on a quota system, with priority determined by performance on national examinations.

The five tracks are: military service (two years), which is optional to continue after completion; logistics service (three years), encompassing work in oil and gas, railroads, and general industrial labour, with priority placement in the sector afterward; civic service (two years), encompassing white-collar, administrative, and service-sector work, with priority placement afterward; the police academy (one year), which leads directly to a career in the national police force; and the Continued Education Programme (four years), which permits immediate enrolment in university.

Each sector submits annual requests for a specific number of participants from the eligible pool. Higher-scoring students receive priority in selecting their preferred track; lower-scoring students may not receive their preferred placement. Students with disabilities are still obligated to participate, as the BWP is regarded as a right of service, but are accommodated according to their circumstances.

The BWP has historically been a major driver of low unemployment and structured job placement. As Burawa's youth population has declined, however, the programme's capacity to fill labour needs has diminished, particularly in the logistics sector, where immigrant workers under the [[Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)|Temporary Worker Program]] have increasingly supplemented domestic labour.

=== Temporary Worker Program ===

{{main|Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)}}

The [[Temporary Worker Program Act of Burawa (2008)|Temporary Worker Program]] permits foreign workers to enter Burawa on renewable six-month contracts, with a maximum duration of three years. The programme reached a peak of approximately 2.4 million workers in 2015 and has since declined to approximately 1.5 million. Workers are drawn primarily from [[Enfolo]] and [[Tamazgha]].

== Finance ==

=== Banking ===

The Burawi banking system comprises state and private institutions. The [[Bank of Burawa]] serves as the central bank and regulator. A state consumer bank handles personal banking services for the general population, and a state commercial bank manages investments and commercial lending. Private corporate banking and private investment banking are permitted, though the state provides no liability protection for investment losses. Regional cooperative credit unions provide community-level banking services.

=== Stock exchanges ===

Burawa operates two stock exchanges. The [[Mazar Stock Exchange]] (MSX) is the primary venue for trading shares in private companies; [[Sare]] is among the most prominent listings. The [[Samara Securities Exchange]] serves as the market for government bonds and securities.

=== Currency ===

The [[Burawi Hanti]] (HAN; ISO code BUR) is the national currency. The Hanti has ancient origins, having evolved from the iron-bar currency system that preceded coinage in the region. It is a floating currency that is softly pegged to the [[International dollar]] at a ratio of approximately four Hanti to one International dollar. The peg was established following the [[Great Adonian War]], when the International dollar was introduced in 1948 under the [[Treaty of Calatayud]].

The Hanti is subject to imported inflation arising from fluctuations in the International dollar. The [[Adonian Monetary Authority]] (AMA), which administers the International dollar, maintains a regional office in [[Mazar]].

== Trade ==

Burawa maintains a {{w|trade surplus}}, exporting more than it imports. The principal export categories are manufactured goods, agricultural products, oil and minerals, and hardware. The principal imports include screens and displays, consumer goods, and raw materials not available domestically.

The [[Import-Export Bank of Burawa]] handles all collective international trade, serving as the intermediary between domestic producers and foreign markets.

[[Volisania]] is Burawa's largest trading partner by a considerable margin. Other significant partners include [[Galwa]], [[Breisland]], [[Merica]], the [[United Midlands]], [[Balisca]], [[Lacashe]], [[Daras]], and [[Zong]]. Technology exports are concentrated among Kaftian neighbours, while hardware is exported globally.

== Taxation ==

The Burawi tax system is distinguished by the absence of a general {{w|sales tax}} or {{w|value-added tax}} and by the exemption of public sector employees from income tax.

Government revenue is derived from several sources. Profits from state-owned enterprises, particularly in the oil, mining, construction, and energy sectors, constitute the largest single revenue stream. Private sector workers are subject to a progressive income tax with a top marginal rate of approximately twenty-five to thirty per cent. Private companies pay a flat corporate tax at a moderate rate. A luxury tax, ranging from eight to fifty per cent, is levied on designated luxury goods including watches, alcohol, and tobacco. A capital gains tax applies to investment returns. Trade tariffs and customs duties are levied on imports and exports.

The exemption of public sector employees from income tax is intended to offset the lower nominal wages in state employment. Combined with free housing and healthcare, the effective compensation of public sector workers approaches or exceeds that of private sector equivalents in many industries.

== Sovereign wealth fund ==

The [[National Reserve Fund]] is Burawa's {{w|sovereign wealth fund}}, financed primarily by oil and gas revenues. The fund's value is estimated at between $500 billion and $1 trillion. The fund was depleted during the Crisis of 1962, when the government sold state land in Mazar and drew on reserves to stabilise the economy; it has since been rebuilt through sustained commodity revenues and fiscal discipline.

== Energy ==

Burawa has pursued a deliberate energy transition since the 1960s. The initial motivation was economic: the heavy dependence on oil and gas revenues during the Malaise Era (1948–1962) exposed the economy to commodity price volatility, and diversification was considered essential for long-term stability. Environmental considerations were incorporated into energy policy from the 1990s onward.

The current energy mix includes hydroelectric power, nuclear energy, solar and other renewable sources, and oil and gas. The [[Dimar]] hydroelectric project, funded in part by foreign investment through the [[National Northern Electric Company]], is among the largest infrastructure investments in the energy sector. The government's stated objective is to reduce dependence on oil, gas, and coal through the expansion of nuclear and renewable energy capacity.

== Transportation ==

The Burawi transportation network is distinguished by its extensive rail infrastructure, a legacy of the competing railroad companies that built tracks across the country during the [[Early Republic Era of Burawa|Early Republic]] and Industrial eras. Freight transport remains heavily dependent on rail. [[BuRail|Burawa Railways]] (BuRail) operates the state-owned national rail network.

Road infrastructure exists alongside the rail network. Public transit is classified as an essential industry and is state-operated. [[Dawati]] serves as the national airline, with [[Mazar Airlines]] operating as a grandfathered private carrier.

== Housing and welfare ==

{{main|Housing in Burawa}}

The Burawi state provides housing, healthcare, and education at no direct cost to residents. These provisions are guaranteed by the [[Constitution of Burawa]] and represent the most significant factor in the gap between the country's nominal and purchasing-power-parity GDP valuations.

The [[Housing in Burawa|state housing system]] provides permanent accommodation to all legal residents over the age of eighteen. Housing is state-owned, with the exception of [[Mazar]], where private property ownership is permitted. The system is administered by the [[Ministry of Housing]] in conjunction with the [[Central Construction Group of Burawa|CCG]] and the [[National Northern Burawi Construction Group|NNBCG]], which manage the majority of residential construction.

== Mazar ==

{{main|Mazar}}

[[Mazar]] occupies a distinctive position in the Burawi economy as the only city in which private property ownership is permitted. The exception originated in agreements made during the [[Great Adonian War]], when the government granted property rights to loyalist capitalists, and was formalised during the Crisis of 1962, when the government sold state land in Mazar to raise funds and pay reparations.

Mazar functions as the financial centre for the private sector, with the [[Mazar Stock Exchange]] and numerous private banks and investment firms headquartered in the city. [[Iskhal]], by contrast, serves as the centre for state-owned enterprise administration. Housing prices in Mazar are among the highest on the Mesogean coast. The [[Adonian Monetary Authority]] maintains a regional office in the city.

== Informal economy ==

An informal economy operates alongside the formal state and private sectors. The most common forms of informal economic activity include the subletting of state housing, in which residents claim one address but rent their accommodation informally to others; informal secondary employment among immigrant workers, many of whom take one or two additional jobs beyond their primary position; street vending, which is more prevalent in rural areas and in regions such as [[Dimar]]; and the resale of luxury goods, which is driven by Burawa's high luxury tax and creates an active secondary market.

== Political debate ==

The extent and direction of economic liberalisation is a subject of political contestation in Burawa. The major political parties occupy a spectrum of positions on the question. The [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] is the most vocal advocate of liberalisation and free trade. The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] supports cautious liberalisation combined with selective nationalisation. The [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] favours free trade and is open to liberalisation. The [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] seeks to maintain a balance between public and private enterprise. The [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]] opposes liberalisation. The [[Christian Socialist Union]] advocates protectionism and opposes liberalisation.

== International economic relations ==

Burawa is an active member of the [[Adonian Community]] and participates in its economic institutions. The [[Burawi Hanti]] has been pegged to the [[International dollar]] since 1948, when the currency was introduced under the [[Treaty of Calatayud]]. The [[Adonian Monetary Authority]] maintains a regional office in [[Mazar]].

